<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">False Face Society</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The False Face Society is a spiritual and medicinal society of the Haudenosaunee, known especially for its wooden masks. It cures ailments through ritual, with power over illnesses of the teeth, ears and joints, inflammation and nosebleeds. Members join by being cured by the society or by dreaming that they have the power to cure; they were men, but the society's leader was always a woman. According to Haudenosaunee oral tradition, the masks represent a being whose face was broken by a mountain and who promised to heal the people. The masks are held to be living, and in 1995 the Haudenosaunee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> condemned their exhibition and called for their return from collectors and museums.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In a version told among the Six Nations, the Creator, walking over the newly made earth, met a stranger who claimed to have made it himself. They agreed that whoever could move a distant mountain farther was the true creator, and turned their backs while each tried. The stranger moved it a little; when the Creator's turn came, the stranger looked round too soon and struck his face on the mountain, which bent his nose and left one side of his face crooked.</p><p>Rather than be sent from the earth, the stranger promised to turn aside storms with his cane and to heal the sick whenever people called on him, burning tobacco and preparing corn mush, with masks carved in his image. They would call him grandfather. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> account he is Ethihsó·dah, 'our grandfather', or Hadó'ih, sent to live in caves and deep forests and known as Old Broken Nose. Haudenosaunee believe that the being still protects them, redirecting fierce winds and healing the ill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Each mask is held to embody the spirit and his power. The masks are usually of basswood, sometimes white pine, poplar or maple, with deep-set eyes accented by metal, crooked noses and hair of horse tail; before Europeans brought horses, corn husks and buffalo hair were used. They are painted red or black. The society holds its main ritual twice a year, in spring and autumn, with smaller ones at the Midwinter Festival and in homes on request: masked members go through the houses of the community to drive away sickness and evil spirits, and the people then gather at the longhouse, where tobacco is burned, the masks' power is renewed and corn mush is shared.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of the Haudenosaunee opposes the sale of the masks to collectors and museums, holding that the society is sacred and not to be shared with those outside it or the nation. Its policies of 1995 prohibit the sale, exhibition and pictures of the masks and reject outsiders' right to present the medicine societies' beliefs and duties. Several Haudenosaunee governments have pressed for the masks' return, and the National Museum of the American Indian in Washington has returned many. Some Haudenosaunee artists, who rely on selling masks, contest the condemnation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “False Face Society” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fraternal medicine societies, of which the <b>False Face Society</b> is the best known, conduct healing rituals.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The medicine societies, brotherhoods entered by formal initiation, held curing rites: in the best known, the <b>False Face Society</b>, masked dancers sought to frighten away the spirits believed to cause illness.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li></ul></section>
